(vector (map (:registry/operating-states (vector :intake :propose :approve :execute :audit)), (:actor/wave 2), (:registry/required-technologies (vector :robotics :identity :audit-ledger)), (:actor/scope-excluded-ops (vector)), (:actor/robotics? true), (:adr/related (vector "adr-2607121000-cloud-itonami-global-isic-isco-reverse-toposort-plan" "adr-2792005412-cloud-itonami-isco-5412-police-officers-coverage" "adr-2799005413-cloud-itonami-isco-5413-prison-guards-coverage")), (:adr/status :accepted), (:actor/scope-excluded-terms (vector "initiate the locomotive movement" "finalize the locomotive movement" "engage the throttle to move the train" "release the brake to depart" "finalize the brake application" "override the stop signal" "depart against the stop signal" "authorize departure against a stop signal" "clear the track without signal authority" "override the signal to proceed")), (:adr/id 2799008311), (:phase/rollout "{0 {:label \"read-only\", :writes #{}, :auto #{}}, 1 {:label \"assisted-logging\", :writes #{:log-service-record}, :auto #{}}, 2 {:label \"assisted-coordination\", :writes #{:log-service-record :schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-maintenance-order}, :auto #{}}, 3 {:label \"supervised-auto\", :writes #{:log-service-record :schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-maintenance-order :flag-safety-concern}, :auto #{:log-service-record :schedule-crew-operation}}}"), (:actor/domain :transport/locomotive-crew-scheduling), (:actor/proposal-ops (vector :log-service-record :schedule-crew-operation :flag-safety-concern :coordinate-maintenance-order)), (:actor/hard-checks (vector :no-driver :license-not-verified :no-actuation :op-not-allowed :scope-exclusion :missing-assignment :unknown-assignment :assignment-wrong-driver :missing-service-log)), (:registry/maturity :implemented), (:actor/isco-08 "8311"), (:actor/always-escalate-ops (vector :flag-safety-concern)), (:adr/doc_type "adr"), (:operation/nodes (vector :intake :advise :govern :decide :request-approval :commit :hold)), (:operation/entry-point :intake), (:adr/body "# ADR 2799008311: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 8311 Locomotive Engine Drivers administrative/logistics scheduling coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 8311\n-- Locomotive Engine Drivers** only.\n\nThis is a retry of a task that a prior agent began in an earlier session\nand was cut off mid-task by a session rate limit: that prior run had\nalready created the `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8311` GitHub\nrepository (default `gh repo create` boilerplate only: `.gitignore`,\n`CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, `GOVERNANCE.md`, `LICENSE`, no\nactor source) and, in the same scratch workspace, had produced a complete\nactor implementation and doc set but had not yet pushed it, promoted the\nregistry entry, or written this ADR. This retry independently re-verified\nthe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"8311\" ...}` entry\nagainst a fresh clone before treating any of that prior work as reusable\n(`clojure -M -e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/\nget-occupation \\\"8311\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Locomotive Engine Drivers\" --\nconfirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior to this ADR the entry's\n`:maturity` was `:spec` with `:required-technologies [:robotics :identity\n:audit-ledger]` and no `:repo`/`:business-id`. This ADR and its companion\nregistry edit promote the `8311` entry to `:implemented`.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313)\n(Accounting Associate Professionals: `store`/`advisor`/`governor`/\n`actor` split, `langgraph.graph` StateGraph, full doc set --\n`blueprint.edn`, `CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`,\n`GOVERNANCE.md`, `LICENSE` AGPL-3.0, `README.md`, `SECURITY.md`,\n`docs/business-model.md`, `docs/operator-guide.md`), independently\nre-cloned and re-read in full in this session.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8311` as an OSS\ndocumentation/logistics-coordination actor for locomotive engine\ndrivers, following the SAME governed-actor architecture as every prior\n`cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Rail Crew Advisor ⊣\nRailCrewGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph` StateGraph\n(`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned identically to the\nreference's `deps.edn` -- this is a different library coordinate from\nthe `langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated `20-actors/*` actor family),\nwith an independent Governor, human-in-the-loop approval via\n`interrupt-before`, and an append-only audit ledger.\n\n### CRITICAL -- rail-vehicle-operation safety dimension\n\nLocomotive Engine Drivers directly operate trains -- a domain with real\nphysical-safety stakes (collision, derailment). This actor is\ndeliberately **administrative/logistics scheduling ONLY, never direct\nlocomotive-operation authority** -- it must NEVER be designed to\nexercise, simulate exercising, or propose exercising a\nlocomotive-movement/throttle/brake-control decision, a\ntrack-clearance/signal-override decision, or a decision to depart\nagainst a stop signal. These are not merely gated behind escalation --\nthey are structurally absent from the op-allowlist entirely, and are\nadditionally defended in depth by a content-based scope-exclusion rule.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-service-record` -- trip/mileage/incident-report data logging\n  (requires an attached service log; logging without one is a\n  fabricated record, not routine logging)\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- driver-roster/shift scheduling proposal\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface a mechanical-defect/signal-anomaly/\n  fatigue concern, ALWAYS escalates immediately, NEVER auto-commit\n  eligible\n- `:coordinate-maintenance-order` -- locomotive maintenance procurement\n  proposal, escalates above the assignment's registered cost ceiling\n\nThis actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles**: a\nlocomotive-movement/throttle/brake-control decision, a\ntrack-clearance/signal-override decision, or a decision to depart\nagainst a stop signal. These are **structurally absent from the\nop-allowlist entirely**, not merely gated behind escalation -- the\nreference mock advisor's `infer` function only ever emits one of the\nfour allowlist ops (driven directly by the incoming request's `:op`,\nwith the governor's closed-allowlist HARD check as the independent,\nunconditional backstop for any `:op` value outside that set, including\na forged/rogue proposal), and if any code path (a forged proposal, a\nfuture LLM advisor, a misbehaving fork) ever produced an out-of-allowlist\nop such as `:operate-locomotive`, the governor's `:op-not-allowed` HARD\ncheck rejects it unconditionally and permanently\n(`railcrew.governor-test/hard-on-op-not-in-allowlist`).\n\n### Nine HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **No driver / unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to a\n   registered driver record (`:no-driver`). Never trusts the proposal's\n   own claim of who the driver is.\n2. **License not verified** -- the driver's route-license record must be\n   independently `:route-license-verified?` true before ANY action is\n   proposed for that driver (`:license-not-verified`). This directly\n   implements the task's \"driver/route-license record must be\n   independently verified/registered before any action\" invariant.\n3. **No actuation** -- any `:effect` other than `:propose` is a HARD\n   block (`:no-actuation`) -- the governor never dispatches hardware and\n   never operates the locomotive.\n4. **Op not allowed** -- any `:op` outside the closed four-op allowlist\n   is a HARD, PERMANENT block (`:op-not-allowed`). This independently\n   rejects any attempt to slip in a locomotive-movement/throttle/\n   brake-control or signal-override/stop-signal-departure op.\n5. **Scope exclusion** -- independent of the op-allowlist check, any\n   proposal whose free text (`:rationale` or `:detail`) names a\n   finalization/execution action for a locomotive movement,\n   throttle/brake control, signal override or stop-signal departure\n   (`railcrew.governor/scope-exclusion-terms`, e.g. \"initiate the\n   locomotive movement\", \"override the stop signal\", \"depart against\n   the stop signal\") is a HARD, PERMANENT block, evaluated\n   unconditionally on content regardless of `:op` or confidence. This is\n   defense-in-depth: even if a future change somehow widened the\n   allowlist, content-based rejection still independently blocks\n   locomotive-operation language wherever it appears in free text.\n6. **Missing assignment** -- `:log-service-record`,\n   `:schedule-crew-operation` and `:coordinate-maintenance-order` must\n   cite an `:assignment-id` (`:missing-assignment`).\n7. **Unknown assignment** -- the cited assignment must be registered\n   (`:unknown-assignment`).\n8. **Assignment wrong driver** -- the cited assignment must belong to\n   the proposing driver (`:assignment-wrong-driver`).\n9. **Missing service log** -- a `:log-service-record` proposal must have\n   `:service-log-attached?` true before any service record can be\n   logged (`:missing-service-log`) -- logging a trip/mileage/incident\n   record without an attached service log is a fabricated record, not\n   routine logging.\n\n### One always-escalate (SOFT) gate, one threshold gate, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (governor's `always-escalate-ops`). This is the ONLY\n  channel by which the robot may surface a mechanical-defect/\n  signal-anomaly/fatigue observation -- it never resolves the\n  observation itself.\n- a `:coordinate-maintenance-order` proposal whose `:estimated-cost`\n  exceeds the cited assignment's registered `:max-maintenance-cost`\n  (inclusive boundary verified by\n  `railcrew.governor-test/ok-maintenance-order-at-exact-cost-ceiling-boundary`)\n  -- always needs human sign-off, independent of confidence. This is an\n  ESCALATION, not a hard block: an over-ceiling maintenance order (e.g.\n  a locomotive overhaul) is a legitimate business action that just\n  requires sign-off, unlike a locomotive-movement/signal-override\n  decision, which is always a hard, permanent block regardless of cost\n  or confidence.\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"throttle\", \"signal\", \"movement\") can accidentally match inside the\nmock advisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate proposal,\nself-blocking the actor's own happy path -- this is exactly the risk\nthis vertical's own vocabulary (every proposal necessarily discusses\nlocomotive/train/signal concepts to do its scheduling job) could\ntrigger if phrased carelessly. `railcrew.governor/scope-exclusion-terms`\nis phrased exclusively as finalization/execution ACTIONS (\"initiate the\nlocomotive movement\", \"engage the throttle to move the train\",\n\"override the stop signal\", \"depart against the stop signal\"), never\nbare nouns, and\n`railcrew.advisor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-scope-exclusion`\nis a dedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed\nallowlist, driven through the default mock advisor for a well-formed\nrequest, never trips the governor's `:scope-exclusion` rule. Green on\nfirst run against the fresh re-clone re-verified in this session.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"8311\" ...}` entry promoted\n  from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id` set to the\n  real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8311` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-8311`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented count\n  bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary` immediately before the edit, to\n  account for concurrent sibling landings in this same retry batch, not\n  assumed from a stale prior count).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 22 tests, 48 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's `:test/counts`\n  and in the task report, independently re-verified against a\n  brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just the prior\n  session's leftover working copy).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies` stacks.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to\n  tx-data\") -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string\n  blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n- `CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md`/`SECURITY.md`/`GOVERNANCE.md`/`CONTRIBUTING.md`\n  say \"cloud-itonami organization\" for private-contact routing, matching\n  the factually-correct org name the repository is actually published\n  under.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-locomotive-movement` or\n  `:approve-signal-override` that is merely gated behind\n  `always-escalate-ops` (the shape `cloud-itonami-isco-3313` uses for\n  its own over-ceiling posting op, or the shape a naive reading of\n  \"gate high-stakes actions behind human approval\" might suggest):\n  rejected outright per the explicit task guidance -- an always-escalate\n  op still means the *capability* to finalize a movement/throttle/\n  brake/signal decision exists in the code path and is merely deferred\n  to a human gate. For a decision this severe (collision, derailment),\n  the capability itself must not exist. The only op related to safety\n  escalation is `:flag-safety-concern`, which surfaces the need for\n  human attention and never itself decides, authorizes, or defers a\n  locomotive-operation action for later automatic execution.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"throttle\" \"brake\"\n  \"signal\" \"movement\"]`): rejected outright per the task's explicit\n  worked description of this fleet-known self-trip bug class and its\n  worked examples of the correct action-phrase form -- this actor's own\n  domain necessarily discusses locomotive/train/signal concepts to do\n  its scheduling job, so a bare-noun list would risk self-blocking the\n  actor's own legitimate proposals (verified empirically green by the\n  dedicated regression test, not merely asserted).\n- A numeric confidence-only gate for `:log-service-record` /\n  `:schedule-crew-operation` / `:coordinate-maintenance-order` (no\n  content-based scope-exclusion check): rejected as insufficient --\n  confidence measures the advisor's certainty about the *proposal it\n  chose to make*, not whether that proposal's free text smuggles\n  forbidden locomotive-operation content, which is exactly why the\n  scope-exclusion check exists as an independent, content-based,\n  permanent HARD block regardless of confidence.\n- Treating the driver record and the route-license verification as a\n  single boolean on the request itself (trusting the proposal's own\n  claim): rejected -- the governor resolves the driver record\n  independently from the store and checks\n  `:route-license-verified?` on that resolved record, never on the\n  incoming request/proposal, so a forged or stale claim on the request\n  cannot bypass verification.\n\n## Itemized safety confirmation\n\nEvery op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is\nsafe under the constraints above:\n\n1. **`:log-service-record`** -- trip/mileage/incident-report data\n   logging only, requires an attached service log\n   (`:missing-service-log` HARD-blocks a bare/fabricated log entry). Not\n   a movement, throttle/brake or signal decision in any respect -- it is\n   a records-management action.\n2. **`:schedule-crew-operation`** -- driver-roster/shift scheduling\n   logistics only. Does not plan, authorize, or draw any conclusion\n   about an actual locomotive-movement/signal decision -- it is a\n   calendar action.\n3. **`:flag-safety-concern`** -- surfaces a mechanical-defect/\n   signal-anomaly/fatigue concern to a human; ALWAYS escalates\n   (governor's `always-escalate-ops`, unconditionally, at every\n   confidence level), NEVER auto-commit eligible. This is the ONLY\n   channel through which a safety observation may be surfaced, and it\n   explicitly never resolves, decides, or acts on anything itself -- the\n   human reviews and acts entirely on their own authority.\n4. **`:coordinate-maintenance-order`** -- locomotive maintenance\n   procurement proposal, gated by cost-ceiling escalation (above the\n   assignment's registered `:max-maintenance-cost` always escalates).\n   Not an operational action of any kind -- it is a purchasing-logistics\n   action for maintenance work, entirely separate from any\n   movement/throttle/brake/signal decision.\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase, resembles\na locomotive-movement/throttle/brake-control decision, a\ntrack-clearance/signal-override decision, or a decision to depart\nagainst a stop signal -- these are structurally absent, not gated,\nconfirmed by: (a) the closed four-op allowlist itself\n(`railcrew.governor/allowed-ops`), (b) the `:op-not-allowed` HARD check\nrejecting any out-of-allowlist op unconditionally and permanently\n(`railcrew.governor-test/hard-on-op-not-in-allowlist`), and (c) a\ncontent-based scope-exclusion check independent of the `:op` field\n(`railcrew.governor/scope-exclusion-terms`,\n`railcrew.governor-test/hard-permanent-block-on-locomotive-movement-scope-exclusion`,\n`railcrew.governor-test/hard-permanent-block-on-stop-signal-departure-scope-exclusion`,\n`railcrew.governor-test/hard-permanent-block-on-signal-override-scope-exclusion-cannot-be-escalated`),\nnone of which can be overridden by confidence, cost, or any other field\n-- all independently proven by the governor unit tests listed above and\nthe dedicated self-trip regression test\n(`railcrew.advisor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-scope-exclusion`)\nproving the actor's own legitimate proposals never trigger these\nchecks by accident.\n"), (:adr/date "2026-07-17"), (:actor/cost-threshold-escalate-ops "{:coordinate-maintenance-order :assignment-max-maintenance-cost}"), (:test/counts "{:total 22, :assertions 48, :failures 0, :errors 0}"), (:actor/coordination? true), (:actor/name "Locomotive Crew Scheduling & Maintenance Coordination Service"), (:db/id -1), (:test/coverage (vector :governor-hard-checks :advisor-shapes :integration :store-contract :scope-exclusion-self-trip-regression :driver-verification :assignment-verification :closed-op-allowlist :maintenance-cost-threshold-escalation)), (:registry/business-id "cloud-itonami-isco-8311"), (:registry/repo "https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8311"), (:adr/scope (vector :kotoba-lang/occupation :cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8311)), (:test/simulator :railcrew.actor-test), (:adr/title "cloud-itonami-isco-8311 — Locomotive Engine Drivers Administrative/Logistics Scheduling Coverage"), (:adr/source-format "edn"), (:adr/references (vector 2607121000)), (:actor/id :cloud-itonami-isco-8311)))
